          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following patients has an uncomplicated urinary tract infection?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following patients has an uncomplicated urinary tract infection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>A 24-year-old non-pregnant woman with acute cystitis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A healthy, non-pregnant young woman with cystitis is the classic uncomplicated UTI — no structural, functional, or immunological complicating factor is present.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A 55-year-old man with dysuria and frequency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Male sex alone classifies a UTI as complicated, because it implies a possible underlying structural or functional abnormality such as prostatic enlargement.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A pregnant woman with dysuria and urgency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Pregnancy is a complicating factor — hormonal ureteric dilatation can allow bacteriuria to progress to pyelonephritis in up to 40% of untreated cases.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A diabetic woman with an indwelling catheter and fever</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Both diabetes (impaired immunity, glycosuria) and an indwelling catheter are complicating factors that increase the risk of treatment failure and serious complications.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A UTI is classified as complicated when it occurs alongside pregnancy, diabetes, catheterisation, immunosuppression, structural abnormalities, renal failure, or male sex. Only a healthy, non-pregnant woman with simple cystitis qualifies as uncomplicated."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. An uncomplicated UTI occurs in a healthy, non-pregnant woman without structural abnormalities, catheters, immunosuppression, or renal failure."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
