          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A catheterised patient's urine culture grows 5,000 colony-forming units per mill</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A catheterised patient's urine culture grows 5,000 colony-forming units per millilitre of Klebsiella pneumoniae. How should this result be interpreted?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Contamination — repeat the specimen</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Catheter specimens carry minimal contamination risk, so a lower significance threshold applies. At 5,000 CFU/mL, this growth is five times the catheter threshold of 10³ CFU/mL.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Significant bacteriuria requiring clinical evaluation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. For catheter specimens, the significance threshold is ≥10³ CFU/mL (1,000), far lower than the 10⁵ for midstream urine, because contamination from periurethral flora is bypassed. 5,000 CFU/mL clearly exceeds this threshold.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Insignificant growth — observe and reculture in 48 hours</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. This count already exceeds the catheter threshold of ≥10³ CFU/mL. Waiting and repeating risks allowing the infection to progress.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Colonisation only — no treatment needed unless symptomatic</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Growth above the specimen-specific threshold constitutes significant bacteriuria, not mere colonisation. The catheter threshold (≥10³ CFU/mL) is deliberately set low because these specimens have minimal contamination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "For catheter specimens, significant bacteriuria begins at ≥10³ CFU/mL (1,000), not the 10⁵ used for midstream clean-catch urine. This lower threshold reflects the reduced contamination risk of catheter-collected specimens."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The catheter specimen threshold of ≥10³ CFU/mL reflects the lower contamination risk — 5,000 CFU/mL is significant and warrants clinical evaluation and appropriate treatment."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
