          "label": "<p>A patient presents with a painless genital ulcer followed weeks later by a symme</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with a painless genital ulcer followed weeks later by a symmetrical rash involving the palms and soles. Which organism is most likely responsible?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neisseria gonorrhoeae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Gonorrhoea typically presents with purulent urethral discharge and dysuria, not a painless genital ulcer or a disseminated rash.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Herpes simplex virus type 2</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Genital herpes causes painful vesicles and ulcers in clusters on an erythematous base, not a single painless ulcer, and does not produce a palm-and-sole rash.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Treponema pallidum</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A painless genital ulcer (primary chancre) followed by a disseminated rash characteristically involving the palms and soles is the hallmark progression from primary to secondary syphilis, caused by Treponema pallidum.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chlamydia trachomatis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Chlamydia typically causes a mucopurulent urethral or cervical discharge, or is entirely asymptomatic. It does not produce a painless chancre or a palm-and-sole rash.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A painless genital ulcer (chancre) followed by a symmetrical rash on the palms and soles is pathognomonic of syphilis (Treponema pallidum) — primary stage progressing to secondary stage."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. The painless chancre → palm-and-sole rash sequence is the classic progression from primary to secondary syphilis caused by Treponema pallidum."
                }
              ],
